# Paths excluded from the pattern-shape checks in scripts/verify.sh.
#
# Each line is a substring matched against the file path. Comments and blank
# lines are ignored. This does NOT exclude anything from the literal-credential
# scan — a real key committed to a test file is still a real key.
#
# Why these entries exist: a security test has to write down the insecure
# pattern in order to prove the control against it, and a detection rule has to
# write it down in order to match it. Reporting those as findings would mean a
# suite that proves a control works reads identically to a codebase missing the
# control, and a checker that cries wolf on its own tests stops being run.
#
# Add a path here only with a reason. If you cannot write the reason, the
# finding is probably real.

# CECC's own test suites. They contain deliberate fixtures: a service-role key
# behind a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, an empty catch block, dangerouslySetInnerHTML
# with interpolated request data. Each one exists so a rule can be shown to
# catch it, and several assert that it IS caught.
cecc/packages/core/test/
cecc/packages/ml/test/

# CECC's detection rules. These files are the regexes that find the patterns,
# so they necessarily contain them. They are server-side Node modules with no
# JSX and no environment access.
cecc/packages/core/src/rules/

# The engineering system's own test directories, for the same reason: security
# tests under tests/security/ exist to attempt the thing they name.
tests/security/
